X-CLIENT under blink: connect WORKS, server-accept + handshake is the final blocker (2026-05-29, ~50 CI cycles). The patched GL-less Xvfb SERVES under blink in-page (PX-smoke EXIT TIMEOUT serving, witnessed). Concurrent X client via blink-core runConcurrent (2-VM cooperative scheduler) + blinkenlib vm_current/vm_set/vm_spawn (distinct (m,s), shared MEMFS+g_socks). FIXED: preempt trampoline (blink-core.js setTimeout resume), abstract AF_UNIX names preserved (blink xlat.c, strnlen dropped them), VM-scoped unix-socket entries via vmid (unixsock_shim; close() no longer frees other VMs listener), first concurrent slice uses _blinkenlib_continue not _run. RESULT: client connect path=@/tmp/.X11-unix/X99 now FINDS the server listener (shared g_socks @0x135420, server vmid=1, 2 listeners) -> connect succeeds. REMAINING BLOCKER: server NEVER calls accept() (no [unixsock] accept trace) -> xdpyinfo unable to open display. Root issue = cooperative multitasking with BLOCKING syscalls: a blocking poll/epoll_wait/read freezes the single wasm thread so the 2 VMs cant interleave through the X handshake. Tried forcing poll/epoll_wait non-blocking (timeout 0) -- did NOT make the server accept AND breaks client blocking reads (returns EAGAIN/0 -> client treats as EOF). The CORRECT fix is blocking syscalls that YIELD: when a guest poll/epoll_wait/read would block, PREEMPT the VM (return to scheduler), let the other VM run, then RESUME + retry when ready -- i.e., convert blocking syscalls into cooperative yield points (not return-immediately). This is a substantial blink scheduler feature (the worker-scheduling PRD). Alternatively run server+client in SEPARATE web workers with shared SAB memory + a shared socket bridge. blink HEAD a13b6ce has the (to-revert) non-blocking poll. The X SERVER + connect are SOLID; the handshake needs the yield-scheduler. Files: blink/{unixsock_shim,epoll_shim,syscall(SysPoll)}.c, blink-core.js runConcurrent.